MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:30Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:30Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:30Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 87\n1: ---\n2: name: quality-auditor\n3: description: 'Code quality gatekeeper and auditor. Enforces strict quality gates, resolves the AI verification gap, and evaluates codebases across 12 critical dimensions with evidence-based scoring. Use when auditing code quality, reviewing AI-generated code, scoring codebases against industry standards, or enforcing pre-commit quality gates. Use for quality audit, code review, codebase evaluation, security assessment, technical debt analysis.'\n4: license: MIT\n5: metadata:\n6:   author: oakoss\n7:   version: '1.1'\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # Quality Auditor\n11: \n12: ## Overview\n13: \n14: Evaluates tools, frameworks, systems, and codebases against the highest industry standards across 12 weighted dimensions. Produces evidence-based scores, identifies anti-patterns, and generates prioritized improvement roadmaps. Applies extra scrutiny to AI-generated code through the verification gap protocol, ensuring velocity does not compromise integrity.\n15: \n16: **When to use:** Auditing code quality, reviewing AI-generated code, scoring codebases against industry benchmarks, enforcing pre-commit quality gates, comparing tools or frameworks, assessing technical debt.\n17: \n18: **When NOT to use:** Quick code reviews without scoring, style-only linting (use a linter), feature implementation, routine PR reviews that do not require a full audit.\n19: \n20: ## Quick Reference\n21: \n22: | Dimension            | Weight | What to Evaluate                                                    |\n23: | -------------------- | ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n24: | Code Quality         | 10%    | Structure, patterns, SOLID, duplication, complexity, error handling |\n25: | Architecture         | 10%    | Design, modularity, scalability, coupling/cohesion, API design      |\n26: | Documentation        | 10%    | Completeness, clarity, accuracy, examples, troubleshooting          |\n27: | Usability            | 10%    | Learning curve, installation ease, error messages, ergonomics       |\n28: | Performance          | 8%     | Speed, resource usage, caching, bundle size, Core Web Vitals        |\n29: | Security             | 10%    | OWASP Top 10, input validation, auth, secrets, dependencies         |\n30: | Testing              | 8%     | Coverage (unit/integration/e2e), quality, automation, organization  |\n31: | Maintainability      | 8%     | Technical debt, readability, refactorability, versioning            |\n32: | Developer Experience | 10%    | Setup ease, debugging, tooling, hot reload, IDE integration         |\n33: | Accessibility        | 8%     | WCAG compliance, keyboard nav, screen readers, cognitive load       |\n34: | CI/CD                | 5%     | Automation, pipelines, deployment, rollback, monitoring             |\n35: | Innovation           | 3%     | Novel approaches, forward-thinking design, unique value             |\n36: \n37: ## Audit Phases\n38: \n39: | Phase | Name                  | Purpose                                                           |\n40: | ----- | --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n41: | 0     | Resource Completeness | Verify registry/filesystem parity; audit fails if this fails      |\n42: | 1     | Discovery             | Read docs, examine code, test system, review supporting materials |\n43: | 2     | Evaluation            | Score each dimension with evidence, strengths, and weaknesses     |\n44: | 3     | Synthesis             | Executive summary, detailed scores, recommendations, risk matrix  |\n45: \n46: ## Scoring Scale\n47: \n48: | Score | Rating      | Meaning                                     |\n49: | ----- | ----------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n50: | 10    | Exceptional | Industry-leading, sets new standards        |"}
{"step_index":861,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:23:30Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:23:30Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:23:31Z\nShowing lines 1-50 of 240\n1: ---\n2: title: Verification Gap Protocol\n3: description: Methodology for verifying AI-generated code quality, critic agent patterns, rejection protocols, and risk-based review strategies\n4: tags:\n5:   [\n6:     verification-gap,\n7:     ai-code-review,\n8:     critic-agents,\n9:     rejection-protocol,\n10:     risk-***,\n11:   ]\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Verification Gap Protocol\n15: \n16: AI-assisted development accounts for a significant and growing share of committed code. The bottleneck has shifted from writing code to reviewing, validating, and governing it. This protocol ensures velocity does not compromise integrity.\n17: \n18: ## The Verification Gap\n19: \n20: The verification gap is the distance between what AI-generated code appears to do and what it actually does in production. AI output can produce functions that compile and pass basic tests yet introduce redundancy, fragile logic, security vulnerabilities, or compliance risks.\n21: \n22: **Key risks of unverified AI code:**\n23: \n24: - Compiles and runs but contains subtle logic errors\n25: - Passes unit tests but fails under real-world edge cases\n26: - Introduces architectural drift by not following project conventions\n27: - Creates security vulnerabilities through incomplete input validation\n28: - Adds unnecessary complexity or dead code paths\n29: - Generates plausible but incorrect implementations of business rules\n30: \n31: ## Closing the Gap\n32: \n33: ### 1. Critic Agent Pattern\n34: \n35: Use high-reasoning models to audit the output of faster code-generation models. The critic agent reviews with a different perspective than the generator.\n36: \n37: **Critic agent responsibilities:**\n38: \n39: - Verify contract integrity (does output match interfaces, schemas, types?)\n40: - Check architectural alignment (does it follow project patterns?)\n41: - Validate security sanitization (are inputs validated, secrets protected?)\n42: - Assess performance hygiene (N+1 queries, bundle size, resource usage)\n43: - Confirm cleanliness (no debug artifacts, commented-out code, TODOs)\n44: \n45: **How to implement:**\n46: \n47: ```ts\n48: // Example: structured audit output from a critic agent\n49: type AuditResult = {\n50:   dimension: string;"}
